# CVE-2026-88647 (hostname verification bypass) and CVE-2026-88648 (incomplete X.509
# name-constraint validation) affect libgnutls30 3.7.9-2+deb12u7 — not installed by
# apps/api/Dockerfile itself, part of the source-1 Chrome Recommends chain above (CUPS,
# curl...). Status: affected, no Fixed Version, no Debian bookworm fix published yet
# (`apt-get upgrade` in the Dockerfile already runs). Not reachable from our code: both are
# flaws in GnuTLS's TLS certificate checks, and nothing in this image opens a TLS connection
# through GnuTLS — the API is Node.js (its own bundled OpenSSL) and the FFF scraper drives
# Chrome, which uses its own BoringSSL, to one fixed, trusted HTTPS domain.
CVE-2026-88647
CVE-2026-88648

# CVE-2026-16742 (systemd-homed local privilege escalation via missing
# home-record signature verification) affects libnss-systemd, libpam-
# systemd, libsystemd-shared, libsystemd0, libudev1, systemd, systemd-sysv
# and systemd-timesyncd, part of the source-2 base-image packages above —
# no Debian bookworm fix published yet (the CI scan reports every one of
# them as Status: affected with no Fixed Version, `apt-get upgrade` in the
# Dockerfile already runs and has nothing newer to offer). Separately
# unreachable regardless: systemd-homed is a daemon that only runs when
# systemd is PID 1 and reachable over D-Bus; this container's PID 1 is
# `node dist/main.js` directly (no init system, no systemd, no D-Bus ever
# started), and the sole local account (`app`) is created with plain
# `adduser`/`addgroup` — never a systemd-homed-managed home record for the
# vulnerable signature check to even apply to.
CVE-2026-16742

# CVE-2026-76642, CVE-2026-78408, CVE-2026-78409, CVE-2026-78410 (util-linux
# mount-helper/nsenter/bind-mount privilege issues) affect bsdutils,
# libblkid1, libfdisk1 and libmount1 — part of the same source-2 base-image
# util-linux install already covered above, just newly-assigned CVEs against
# it (installed version 1:2.38.1-5+deb12u3, Status: affected, no Fixed
# Version; re-verified with `apt list --upgradable` inside a fresh
# node:22-bookworm-slim — empty, no Debian bookworm fix published yet).
# Every one of these needs a local, privileged mount/cgroup/nsenter
# operation to trigger — this container never runs `mount`, `nsenter`, or
# any privileged namespace operation; its own entrypoint is `node
# dist/main.js` with no such syscalls anywhere in the app or its deps.
CVE-2026-76642
CVE-2026-78408
CVE-2026-78409
CVE-2026-78410

# CVE-2026-86140 (libxml2 stack-based buffer overflow, HIGH, arbitrary code
# execution) — not in the bare node:22-bookworm-slim base image at all
# (confirmed: `docker run --rm node:22-bookworm-slim dpkg -l` has no
# libxml2), so it's pulled in by Google Chrome's own .deb Recommends chain
# (`playwright install chrome`, source 1 above) the same as the rest of
# that block — grouped separately here only because it's a newer,
# individually-severe finding worth calling out on its own rather than
# silently folding into the bulk list. No Debian bookworm fix published for
# the installed 2.9.14+dfsg-1.3~deb12u6 (`apt-cache policy libxml2` inside a
# fresh node:22-bookworm-slim shows that same build as both Installed-
# candidate and security-channel version — nothing newer to move to).
# Exploiting a libxml2 parser bug needs attacker-controlled XML/SVG content
# actually reaching Chrome's parser — the FFF scraper only ever visits one
# fixed, trusted FFF domain over HTTPS in pure headless mode, never
# attacker- or user-supplied URLs/files, same reachability argument as
# source 1's whole Recommends chain above.
CVE-2026-86140

# CVE-2026-86138, CVE-2026-86139, CVE-2026-86142, CVE-2026-86143,
# CVE-2026-86144 — same package, same installed version
# (2.9.14+dfsg-1.3~deb12u6) and same source as CVE-2026-86140 immediately
# above, just newly-assigned CVE IDs against that unchanged, still-unpatched
# build (re-verified via the CI scan output itself: Status: affected, no
# Fixed Version for all five). Same reachability argument: no attacker- or
# user-supplied XML/SVG content ever reaches Chrome's parser, since the FFF
# scraper only ever visits one fixed, trusted domain over HTTPS.
CVE-2026-86138
CVE-2026-86139
CVE-2026-86142
CVE-2026-86143
CVE-2026-86144

# CVE-2026-74860 (libxml2 double-free/UAF via Python bindings, HIGH) — same
# package, same installed version (2.9.14+dfsg-1.3~deb12u6) and same source
# as CVE-2026-86140 immediately above; re-verified `apt-cache policy
# libxml2` inside a fresh node:22-bookworm-slim — that build is still both
# the Installed-candidate and security-channel version, no Debian bookworm
# fix published. This particular CVE is specifically about libxml2's
# *Python* bindings (python3-libxml2/lxml) — this image installs neither
# (Chrome's Recommends chain pulls the C library only, for its own internal
# XML/SVG parsing), so it's unreachable on top of the reachability argument
# already covering the whole block above.
CVE-2026-74860

# CVE-2026-82049 (Python tarfile module: file modification/content
# disclosure via crafted archives, HIGH) hits libpython3.11-minimal,
# libpython3.11-stdlib, python3.11 and python3.11-minimal, all at the same
# installed version 3.11.2-6+deb12u8, Status: affected, no Fixed Version —
# re-verified `docker run --rm node:22-bookworm-slim dpkg -l | grep python`
# returns nothing, so Python isn't part of the bare base image (unlike
# source 2's util-linux/systemd above); it's pulled in by Chrome's own
# .deb Recommends chain the same as source 1's whole block. This container
# never invokes Python anywhere — the API is Node.js only, and the FFF
# scraper never extracts tar archives (crafted or otherwise) via Python's
# tarfile module or any other means — so the vulnerable code path is never
# reached, same reachability argument as the rest of source 1.
CVE-2026-82049

# CVE-2026-88806 (libX11: heap-based buffer overflow via a malicious X server, HIGH) and
# CVE-2026-88807 (libXrender: code injection via heap overflow in RenderQueryPictFormats,
# HIGH) hit libx11-6/libx11-data/libx11-xcb1 (installed 2:1.8.4-2+deb12u2) and libxrender1
# (installed 1:0.9.10-1.1) — Status: affected, no Fixed Version for either. Same category as
# libexpat1 above: all three are explicitly listed in apps/api/Dockerfile's own `apt-get
# install --no-install-recommends` block, X11 client libraries headless Chrome links against
# — not pulled in incidentally. Re-verified 2026-09-24: a fresh `docker run node:22-bookworm-
# slim` has none of them installed (genuinely this Dockerfile's own explicit dependency, not
# the base image), and `apt-cache policy libx11-6 libxrender1` in it shows those exact
# versions as both the current candidate and what's already installed — nothing newer for
# `apt-get upgrade` (already run in the Dockerfile) to move to; no Debian bookworm fix
# published yet. Unreachable regardless: both need a malicious X server or attacker-supplied
# font/picture-format data actually reaching Chrome's X11/Xrender code path — the FFF scraper
# runs Chrome in pure headless mode (no X server, ever) and only ever visits one fixed,
# trusted FFF domain over HTTPS, never attacker- or user-supplied content.
CVE-2026-88806
CVE-2026-88807

# CVE-2026-94286 (libXtst: Denial of Service via out-of-bounds read in the RECORD extension's
# reply parser, HIGH) hits libxtst6 (installed 2:1.2.3-1.1) — Status: affected, no Fixed
# Version. Same category as libx11-6/libxrender1 immediately above: explicitly listed in
# apps/api/Dockerfile's own `apt-get install --no-install-recommends` block, an X11 client
# library headless Chrome links against — not pulled in incidentally (confirmed absent from a
# fresh node:22-bookworm-slim). Re-verified 2026-10-02 inside the built image: `apt-cache
# policy libxtst6` shows that exact version as both Installed and Candidate, nothing newer for
# `apt-get upgrade` (already run in the Dockerfile) to move to; no Debian bookworm fix
# published yet. Unreachable regardless: the RECORD extension records X input events from a
# live X server — the FFF scraper runs Chrome in pure headless mode, no X server ever started,
# so this code path never executes.
CVE-2026-94286

# CVE-2026-96889 (librsvg: use-after-free when an SVG's XML includes duplicated entities,
# HIGH) hits librsvg2-2 and librsvg2-common (installed 2.54.7+dfsg-1~deb12u1) — Status:
# affected, no Fixed Version. Re-verified 2026-09-25: in a fresh `docker run
# node:22-bookworm-slim`, `apt-cache policy librsvg2-2 librsvg2-common` shows Candidate ==
# that same version (bookworm and bookworm-security alike), so no patched package exists to
# upgrade to; and neither is in the base image (`dpkg -l | grep rsvg` there is empty) nor in
# apps/api/Dockerfile's own explicit package list — they come in as a dependency of the
# GTK/icon-theme libraries the headless-Chrome libraries pull in. Not reachable: no code of
# ours calls librsvg; the one place user-supplied images are decoded (avatar upload,
# users.controller.ts) goes through `sharp`, which ships its own libvips inside node_modules
# (@img/sharp-libvips-linux-x64), not the system librsvg; and the FFF scraper's headless
# Chrome renders SVG with its own engine, over HTTPS to one fixed, trusted FFF domain.
CVE-2026-96889

# brace-expansion DoS in OUR app's dependency tree (CVE-2026-14257 and its
# mitigation-bypass follow-up) is fixed, not ignored — see the root
# package.json `overrides` entry pinning the minimatch@3.1.5 instance to
# brace-expansion@1.1.18 (same 1.x API, no rewrite needed).

# libcups2 (Debian 12 bookworm-slim base image, apps/api/Dockerfile) —
# CVE-2026-55453: arbitrary code execution via newline injection in printer
# status messages. Not our code or a direct dependency: libcups2 is only
# installed as one of Google Chrome's standard headless runtime libraries
# (alongside libnss3, libatk1.0-0, etc., for Playwright's `channel: 'chrome'`
# — see the Dockerfile's own comment above that apt-get install block), never
# for actual printing. No CUPS daemon runs in this container, no printer is
# attached, and this API never processes a print job or printer status
# message of any kind — the exploit path doesn't exist here. Verified
# 2026-10-18: Debian's own candidate version for bookworm is still
# 2.4.2-3+deb12u9, identical to what's installed — no security update
# published yet (Trivy's own "Fixed Version" column is empty too). Revisit
# by checking `apt-cache policy libcups2` against debian:bookworm-slim, or
# just rebuilding the image, once Debian ships a patched version.
CVE-2026-55453

# undici@6.29.0 (CVE-2026-109869: Denial of Service via an unhandled stream
# error on excessive content; CVE-2026-109383: certificate validation bypass via TLS
# options dropped in Agent; CVE-2026-87018: Denial of Service via TLS renegotiation with a
# mismatched certificate — all HIGH, all fixed at 6.29.1 / 7.30.1 / 8.12.2) — vendored INSIDE the globally-installed
# npm binary (the Dockerfile's `npm install -g npm@latest`, which refreshes
# npm's own bundled dependencies), unfixable by us, not our dependency
# tree: `npm ls undici --omit=dev --workspace=api` is empty and nothing in apps/api
# references undici. The container runs
# `node dist/main.js`, never npm, so the vendored copy isn't loaded by the API
# process. Verified 2026-10-24: the very latest npm release (12.2.1) still
# vendors undici 6.29.0 — the fix (6.29.1 / 7.30.1 / 8.12.2) isn't shipped by
# any npm release yet, so upgrading npm can't clear it. Revisit by re-packing
# the newest npm (`npm pack npm@latest` and reading
# node_modules/undici/package.json) and dropping this entry once it vendors
# 6.29.1+. The undici TLS findings (109383, 87018) need an outbound TLS connection made
# through that vendored copy — npm is never run at runtime and the API process never loads
# it. The same scan's body-parser and multer findings were NOT ignored —
# those are in our tree and were fixed by bumping them (body-parser 2.4.0,
# multer 2.5.0 via the root package.json override).
CVE-2026-109869
CVE-2026-109383
CVE-2026-87018
